Z||000000||Motorhead||05-07-99||05:27 PM||||Had a guy tell me that these 4-cylinder engines we all love so much like to talk to you through the timing gears! My car doesn't seem to have developed this talent yet, but is it true that the weak link in the "chain" is the timing gears?  <br>
Z||000001||theogre||05-07-99||08:08 PM||||"Iron Duke" is some what unique in that it doesn't use an overhead cam. It is not unique at all in the composition of it's timing gears.<p>Like many other engines, the Fiero 4cyl uses a steel drive gear, and a composit driven gear. The composit gear simply wears out over time. (for comparison... Ford small block v8 use this setup with a chian thrown in.)<p>OHC 4's have problems of their own.... commonly the composit cog belt fails. In a Ford Escort prior to a certain year this belt failing meant major engine damage. In other cars the thing just don't run.
Z||000002||Motorhead||05-07-99||08:58 PM||||Translated into laymans terms, "intermingling of pistons and valve train equals engine damage!!"<p>I'm assuming the timing gear sets I see offered for sale in the popular catalogs replaces the composite gear with one of greater strength. Steel maybe?
